A palette a program sets wins over every target's own answer.

Unset, html and gtk4 draw code in the default palette and the other three ask
their host's theme. Set through `markup.palette`, every target draws the
palette it was given, which is what lets an application with colors of its own
see them in its code samples.

A provided context is state on every target here, so the color reaches each
emitter as a run-time read rather than the literal the unset case folds to --
which is the half this archive is about. gtk4 writes it into the markup through
`gtk4rt.Foreground`, html onto the element's `style.color`, fyne through the
span's `SetColor`, bubbletea as the color the token's own falls back from, and
android as the color the scheme's role falls back from. A kind the palette
leaves unset keeps the host's answer on all five.

The override reading the context is what reached codegen as a bare
`ContextRead` before: `passContext` rewrites reads only in the components it
lists, and a bodiless library component was listed only when its override
declared state.

deny out/go/gtk4/model.go `<tt>func main</tt>` -- the words are colored; a color the build could not read used to be dropped
deny out/none/html/index.html `setAttribute\("(spanStyle|classStyle|classStyleDark)"` -- a run's style is CSS, never an attribute
deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `Foreground\(lipgloss\.Color\("[0-9]+"\)\)` -- the terminal color is the fallback, not the answer

-- main.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"
import markup "sngl:ui/markup"

output {
    go {
        gtk4
        bubbletea
        fyne
    }
    kotlin {
        android
    }
    none {
        html
    }
}

ui.window #home(title="t", href="/") {
    markup.palette(markup.darkPalette) {
        markup.richText {
            markup.monospace {
                markup.token(kind=markup.Token.keyword) {
                    markup.text("func")
                }
                markup.text(" ")
                markup.token(kind=markup.Token.function) {
                    markup.text("main")
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
